#4471 was filed as one bug with one fix queued, and closed on 5 October. The table below is
built from the reports at render time, and it had been showing two causes since April. The
paragraph that used to stand above it was written once and never re-read.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

<Callout kind="warn">
The row the table marks <code>not this ticket — no ticket of its own</code> is a second bug.
The fix shipped on #4471 did not reach it.
</Callout>

## The two

**`parser`.** Every tenant on file writes the day before the month. The shared parser reads
every date month first, so the two swap. This happens on every channel, for every tenant, and
it is what #4471 describes.

**`hint`.** The `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what
the hint tells them, month first, and the form then reads it the tenant's way, day first. The
date swaps again. This one needed the hint changed, not the parser, so it only reached reports
that came in through `form`, from tenants on `booking-v3`.

## What engineering did

#4471 closed on 5 October with `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. That is the `parser`
cause, and it is fixed.

The `hint` cause closed as won't fix: `booking-v3` is retired. The cause leaves with the form,
not with a fix, and the parser change never touched it. Anyone reading only #4471 would take
"one issue, one fix" at face value and conclude the parser fix covered all of it. It covered
the larger group. The smaller one was ended a different way, and nothing in #4471 says so.

## Why they landed together

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line alone, so
nobody reads the body. A wrong day looks the same from either bug, so both wrote the same
subjects and both merged into the first ticket open. The rule did what it says; the subject
line is just not the thing that separates these two.

The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) that carries the rule is past the desk's own staleness
mark, and it is the page new people on the desk are told to start with.

## Two things that move the counts

**Intake tags are a guess.** `meta.intakeTag` is read off the reporter's browser the moment
the report arrives. The cause depends on the tenant: the locale they write dates in and the
form they run. The `tagged as` column above shows how often the two disagree, and it disagrees
in both directions. A count taken from the tag lands in the wrong place for every one of them.

**The export repeats itself.** The ticket system exports in pages that overlap, so a report
can appear twice under one id. The row count of the export file is higher than the number of
reports. `uniqueById` in `causes.ts` is what makes the two agree.

## Tickets

- `parser` → [wgruszczyk/mega-dev-26-public#1](https://github.com/wgruszczyk/mega-dev-26-public/issues/1), closed as fixed
- `hint` → [wgruszczyk/mega-dev-26-public#2](https://github.com/wgruszczyk/mega-dev-26-public/issues/2), closed as won't fix

Each carries the count the table computes for it, not a number typed by hand.

